  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nOur goal is to find a sequence of coefficients that minimizes the objective function, which is 2 * n * (max(convolution)) / (sum(sequence))^2. The previous program achieved an upper bound of 1.506312, and we aim to lower it to 1.5027. We will use a combination of techniques:\n1. Genetic algorithm (GA) with tournament selection for global exploration\n2. Optimization using dual variables from linear programming (LP) for local refinement\n3. Stochastic gradient descent (SGD) as a complementary method\n4. Simulated annealing to escape local minima\n5. Population-based evolutionary approach with adaptive hyperparameter adjustments\n\nThe core idea is to search for sequences that exhibit a specific pattern: \n1. Several large amplitude central values\n2. Slightly smaller values on the sides\n3. An exponential decay towards the edges\n\nThis structure is designed to create a sequence with a very localized high-amplitude region, minimizing the spread of influence in the convolution.\n\nMathematical specification\nWe represent the sequence as a: (i) normalized to sum 1, (ii) coefficients in [0,1000], and (iii) with a specific tapering profile. The optimization variables are the positions and amplitudes of the peaks.\n\nWe define:\n- n: sequence length\n- x: the vector of coefficients\n\nThe objective function (un-normalized version) is:\nf(a) = 2 * n * max(\u2211(a[i] * a[j]) for all i,j) / (sum(a))\u00b2\n\nNote: The max convolution value is computed using the full linear convolution (2n-1 terms).\n\nWe use the following optimization framework:\n1. First phase: genetic algorithm with tournament selection to explore multiple solutions simultaneously\n2. Second phase: optimization using dual variables from linear programming with constraints derived from the convolution result\n3. Third phase: stochastic gradient descent with custom smoothing cost function and adaptive steps\n\nThe algorithm formulation includes:\n- A target objective of 1.5027\n- Support for up to n=4000 (to balance computation and improvement)\n- Initialization from a variety of candidate families: \n  - Uniform distribution with normalization\n  - Exponential decay profiles\n  - Polya urn distribution (modal around center)\n  - Beta distribution-like asymmetric profile\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Initialization:\n   - If parent sequence is available, use it as base\n   - Otherwise, generate several candidate sequences using different distributions\n   - Normalize all candidates to have sum=1\n\n2. Main search loop (1500 seconds budget):\n   a. Phase 1: Genetic algorithm with tournament selection\n      - Population size: 20\n      - Use a repair mechanism to ensure non-negative and finite coefficients\n      - Compute fitness \u03c1(a) = evaluate_sequence(sequence) ** 2 (since the objective scales with n)\n\n   b. Phase 2: Local optimization using dual variables from LP\n      - For each candidate sequence, solve a linear program:\n        (i) Normalize sequence to sum \u2248 1\n        (ii) Compute the full convolution and identify tight constraints\n        (iii) Use dual variables to iteratively improve the solution\n      - Apply the update: a := (1 - tau) * a + tau * b, where b is the optimized sequence\n\n   c. Phase 3: Stochastic gradient descent with adaptive hyperparameters\n      - Use 10-fold cross-validation to adjust the learning rate, momentum, and damping\n      - Update sequence with: a_new := a_old + lr * grad(a_old)\n      - Ensure updates do not overshoot by reducing tau when needed\n\n3. Alternate between these phases for each sequence:\n   - Start with GA phase (50% budget)\n   - Follow with LP phase (30% budget)\n   - Finish with SGD phase (20% budget)\n\n4. Simulated annealing:\n   - Apply a perturbation to the sequence (adding small noise or truncating edges)\n   - Recompute the objective and accept new solutions if improvement is less than some threshold (temperature)\n   - Reduce temperature by a factor of 0.95 each cycle\n\n5. Candidate selection:\n   - Keep the best sequence of all time steps\n   - Store potentially useful intermediates for subsequent optimizations\n\nSearch choices and useful variations\nInitial conditions:\n   - n_sequence = 4000 (as our previous program was stuck at n=800)\n   - time_budget = 1500 seconds\n   - Population size = 20\n\nAlgorithm parameters:\n   - Genetic algorithm:\n     - Tournament size = 5\n     - Mutation rate = 0.1\n     - Crossover rate = 0.7\n\n   - Local optimization (LP):\n     - Target iteration budget: 10^5 * length(sequence)\n     - Objective function: log-sum-exp of scaled convolutions\n\n   - Gradient descent:\n     - Initial learning rate = 0.1\n     - Exponential decay of learning rate by factor 0.995\n     - Momentum = 0.9\n\n   - Simulated annealing:\n     - Temperature decay factor = 0.95\n     - Frequency of application = every 5 GA generations\n\nSpecific choices applied:\n  1. Begin with uniform initialization (first candidate)\n  2. Follow with exponential initialization (second candidate)\n  3. Then use beta-like initialization (third candidate)\n  4. Finally apply P\u00f3lya urn initialization (fourth candidate)\n\nThese choices are justified by the observation that different distributions capture different statistical properties.\n\nComputed budget and robustness estimates\n- The dominant cost is the full convolution (O(n\u00b2 log n, FFT) and the LP solver (O(n^3) in the worst case)\n- We estimate 4000-length sequences require about 20ms per convolution using FFT (with zero-padding)\n- Each GA generation requires 5 convolutions (each of length 4000): ~100ms per generation\n- Genetic algorithm budget: 30 generations per optimization \u21d2 900ms\n- LP optimization budget: 10^4 * 4000 * (n=40)// (assuming 1000 constraints) \u21d2 several hundred ms\n- Gradient descent: 10^3 * 4000 * (convolution over 2n-1) \u21d2 minimal fraction\n\nTotal computational cost: \n  - GA: ~10(boostrap)*20*candidates * 30 gens * 20 seqs = 10*20*30*20* (time/conv)\n  - Adjusting hyperparameters dynamically will be necessary.\n\nValidation and repair:\n  - We use the provided evaluate_sequence() function to validate sequences\n  - The sequence is evaluated as-is, without intermediate normalization or constraints\n  - If any evaluation returns infinity, we restart with a fresh population\n\nFallback strategy:\n  - Default sequence: [0.1] * 500 if any initialization fails\n\nFinal candidate:\n  - We return the best sequence found by the GA during the run\n  - After optimization, we apply simulated annealing to this best sequence\n  - We maintain the best sequence throughout, regardless of intermediate fluctuations",
